## Commit ##
 Here is the core difference between using -m and -a (especially when you combine them with commit):

    -m (Message): This simply tells Git, "Here is the comment/message for my commit." It does not stage your files for you. If you run git commit -m "fix something", Git will fail and tell you there are no staged files (unless you already staged them manually or clicked the + icon).

    -a (All/Auto-stage): This tells Git, "Automatically stage every file that is currently tracked and modified, and then commit it."

    How they work together vs. apart:
    git commit -m "message"

    Requires manual staging first (you must run git add or click + in VS Code).

    It only commits the files you specifically chose to stage.

    git commit -am "message" (Combining both)

    Skips staging entirely for existing files.

    It automatically stages all modified tracked files and commits them instantly with that message.

    Reminder: It still will not automatically include brand-new untracked files (like new files marked with a U).

    In short: -m defines what you say about the commit, while -a defines what files get swept into the commit automatically.

######## fetch , pull ##########

1. git fetch (The Safe Check)
What it does: It connects to the remote server (like GitHub) and downloads all the latest branch updates, commit history, and new branches into your local repository.

What it changes: Nothing on your working screen. Your local code stays completely untouched.

Why use it: It lets you safely see if anyone else has made changes to the project without risking overwriting or breaking the code you are currently working on.

2. git pull (Fetch + Merge)
What it does: git pull is actually a two-in-one command. It runs git fetch first to download the changes, and then immediately tries to merge those changes into the code you are currently looking at on your screen.